# TYPE net_dhcp_restart_link_up_timeouts_total counter
net_dhcp_restart_link_up_timeouts_total 2453629

# HELP net_dhcp_address_conflicts_total Leased addresses RFC 5227 found already in use by another host, over the whole life of the lease: section 2.1's probes before the address is used and section 2.4's listener afterwards. Healthy-affecting. Moves in conflict_check=wait and =async. In =off the client neither probes nor listens, so this moves only for a conflict reported to the client from outside it, which no code path in this plugin does today.
# TYPE net_dhcp_address_conflicts_total counter
net_dhcp_address_conflicts_total 1942014

# HELP net_dhcp_acd_probes_sent_total RFC 5227 section 2.1.1 ARP Probes sent. READ THIS BEFORE BELIEVING address_conflicts IS ZERO: zero here over a running plugin means no address was ever checked, which is not the same reading as a clean segment (#524). Moves in conflict_check=wait and =async, never in =off.
# TYPE net_dhcp_acd_probes_sent_total counter
net_dhcp_acd_probes_sent_total 4230282

# HELP net_dhcp_acd_announcements_sent_total RFC 5227 section 2.3 ARP Announcements sent, two per address that passed the probe. Moves in conflict_check=wait and =async, never in =off. Read against acd_probes_sent: probes climbing with no announcements means addresses are being checked and none is coming back clean.
# TYPE net_dhcp_acd_announcements_sent_total counter
net_dhcp_acd_announcements_sent_total 5321411

# HELP net_dhcp_acd_conflicts_detected_total Conflicts the DHCP library itself counted. It is the same population as address_conflicts, counted inside the state machine rather than from the events it emitted, and the two are expected to be equal; a difference is a defect in the plugin's event handling, not a property of the segment. Its =off rule is address_conflicts's, not a different one: with no probe and no listener the only thing that can move either counter is a conflict reported to the client from outside it, which no code path in this plugin does today.
# TYPE net_dhcp_acd_conflicts_detected_total counter
net_dhcp_acd_conflicts_detected_total 4640136

# HELP net_dhcp_acd_arp_send_failures_total ARP Probes and Announcements the socket refused. Not healthy-affecting: a refused send is not itself a conflict, but a probe that never went out proves nothing about the address, so a rise turns "no conflict found" into "the question was not asked". Moves in conflict_check=wait and =async, never in =off.
# TYPE net_dhcp_acd_arp_send_failures_total counter
net_dhcp_acd_arp_send_failures_total 6209145

# HELP net_dhcp_parent_link_waits_total Endpoint creations that waited for their parent interface to appear.
# TYPE net_dhcp_parent_link_waits_total counter
net_dhcp_parent_link_waits_total 5247821

# HELP net_dhcp_parent_link_wait_timeouts_total Endpoint creations where the parent interface never appeared inside the wait.
# TYPE net_dhcp_parent_link_wait_timeouts_total counter
net_dhcp_parent_link_wait_timeouts_total 8552828

# HELP net_dhcp_tombstone_write_failures_total Tombstone writes that failed, so the next restart of that container picks a new MAC and address. Healthy-affecting.
# TYPE net_dhcp_tombstone_write_failures_total counter
net_dhcp_tombstone_write_failures_total 7499476

# HELP net_dhcp_tombstone_quarantines_total Times the tombstone file was found unparseable and moved aside as tombstones.json.corrupt-<ts>; every live tombstone on the host was lost with it, so containers restarting in the next TTL window come back with new MACs and addresses. Healthy-affecting.
# TYPE net_dhcp_tombstone_quarantines_total counter
net_dhcp_tombstone_quarantines_total 1780865

# HELP net_dhcp_tombstones_consumed_total Tombstones read back to preserve a container's MAC and address across a restart.
# TYPE net_dhcp_tombstones_consumed_total counter
net_dhcp_tombstones_consumed_total 5670747

# HELP net_dhcp_unsafe_hostnames_rejected_total Container hostnames dropped before reaching the DHCP request because they carried a control character. A legitimate hostname never does, so any rise is deliberate (#692).
# TYPE net_dhcp_unsafe_hostnames_rejected_total counter
net_dhcp_unsafe_hostnames_rejected_total 5543185

# HELP net_dhcp_unsafe_option_values_dropped_total Server-chosen DHCP string values refused before use because they carried a control character, plus option-15 domains truncated at their first space. The DHCP library validates domain-typed options; string-typed ones can carry anything the server put on the wire (#703, #704).
# TYPE net_dhcp_unsafe_option_values_dropped_total counter
net_dhcp_unsafe_option_values_dropped_total 9536334

# HELP net_dhcp_network_options_rejected_total Endpoint operations that met a network's stored options and would not act on them as written: an interface name the kernel would not accept, or a mode this plugin does not implement. DeleteEndpoint counts without refusing, so a rise does not mean nothing was torn down. Not healthy-affecting: refusing is the safe outcome and the operation already fails visibly to Docker. A rise means options persisted before name validation existed, or a hand-edited state directory (#727).
# TYPE net_dhcp_network_options_rejected_total counter
net_dhcp_network_options_rejected_total 5605255

# HELP net_dhcp_dns_propagation_pid_mismatches_total DNS propagations refused because the container PID resolved through Docker no longer belonged to that container. The plugin shares the host PID namespace, so each one is a resolv.conf write that would otherwise have landed in an unrelated host process (#688).
# TYPE net_dhcp_dns_propagation_pid_mismatches_total counter
net_dhcp_dns_propagation_pid_mismatches_total 9341279

# HELP net_dhcp_netns_pid_mismatches_total Sandbox network-namespace opens refused because the container PID resolved through Docker no longer belonged to that container. Each one is a netlink handle, and a root DHCP client, that would otherwise have been bound to an unrelated host process's network namespace.
# TYPE net_dhcp_netns_pid_mismatches_total counter
net_dhcp_netns_pid_mismatches_total 9479108

# HELP net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entries_total Container network namespaces entered through the sandbox key the daemon publishes at /var/run/docker/netns/<key>. This is the denominator for sandbox_pid_fallbacks: zero fallbacks with zero entries here means nothing was opened, not that the key route works.
# TYPE net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entries_total counter
net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entries_total 9128177

# HELP net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entry_failures_total Attempts to enter a container network namespace through the sandbox key that were refused — the key named a directory this plugin does not accept, or the entry could not be opened. Each one falls back to the container PID route; a sustained rise means the /var/run/docker mount is not carrying the daemon's sandbox netns entries on this host.
# TYPE net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entry_failures_total counter
net_dhcp_sandbox_key_entry_failures_total 3507070

# HELP net_dhcp_sandbox_pid_fallbacks_total Endpoints whose network namespace was entered through /proc/<pid>/ns/net after the sandbox key route was refused. That route is why the manifest asks for the host PID namespace and CAP_SYS_PTRACE; a zero here across a host's whole uptime, with sandbox_key_entries non-zero, is the evidence that it was not needed.
# TYPE net_dhcp_sandbox_pid_fallbacks_total counter
net_dhcp_sandbox_pid_fallbacks_total 5585284

# HELP net_dhcp_docker_api_non_get_refusals_total Requests to the Docker API refused before they were sent because their method was not GET. The plugin's whole Docker surface is three read calls, so this stays zero unless code in this process tried to write to the daemon; the socket mount is the grant that makes such a write equivalent to root on the host (#691).
# TYPE net_dhcp_docker_api_non_get_refusals_total counter
net_dhcp_docker_api_non_get_refusals_total 4050535

# HELP net_dhcp_ledger_write_failures_total Lease-ledger writes that failed.
# TYPE net_dhcp_ledger_write_failures_total counter
net_dhcp_ledger_write_failures_total 8412728
